        <div class="faq-list">
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What is a Child Education Cost Calculator?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              A Child Education Cost Calculator is a specialized financial planning tool designed to project the future cost of your child's schooling or higher education by factoring in education inflation, investment compounding, existing savings, and multi-year fee withdrawals.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Why is education inflation higher than normal retail inflation?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Education inflation typically outpaces general consumer CPI by 3% to 6% annually across India, the UAE, and Singapore. This higher rate is driven by rising faculty salaries, campus technology infrastructure, specialized laboratory equipment, administrative compliance, and high competition for accredited, top-tier private and international curricula.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How does the calculator determine required monthly savings (SIP)?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              The calculator first projects each academic year's inflated fee and discounts them back to the start of education to compute the target corpus required at Year T. It then subtracts the compounded future value of your existing savings. Finally, it uses the compound annuity sinking fund formula to determine the exact fixed monthly SIP needed to reach that net target.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What realistic education inflation rate should I assume for India?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              For India, historical National Sample Survey Office (NSSO) and industry benchmarks indicate an average education inflation rate of 8% to 12% annually for private engineering, medical, management, and reputable private K-12 schools. We recommend using 10% as a prudent baseline.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How do school fees work in Dubai and the UAE?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              In Dubai and the UAE, private school tuition increases are regulated by the Knowledge and Human Development Authority (KHDA) via the Education Cost Index (ECI) framework, with annual adjustments typically ranging between 3% and 6%. Public school admission is free for UAE citizens, while eligible expatriates face statutory annual fees around AED 6,000.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How does student status affect university tuition in Singapore?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              In Singapore, the Ministry of Education (MOE) Tuition Grant heavily subsidizes tuition for Singapore Citizens (paying roughly S$8,250 to S$12,000 annually for standard undergraduate degrees). Permanent Residents pay approximately 40% to 50% more, while International Students pay more than double the citizen rate.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">What happens if my expected investment return is zero?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              If you set the expected annual investment return to 0%, the calculator assumes cash hoarding without any capital growth. Your required monthly savings will equal the uncompounded shortfall divided linearly by the total number of months available before education starts.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">How does the calculator treat existing education savings?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Existing savings (such as mutual funds, fixed deposits, Sukanya Samriddhi, or dedicated cash reserves) are compounded forward at your expected return rate until the year education begins. This future accumulated value directly reduces the net target corpus you must fund through new monthly contributions.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Should I plan for additional expenses beyond university tuition?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              Yes. Non-tuition expenses—such as hostel accommodation, food, textbooks, laptop technology, medical insurance, transport, and entrance coaching—frequently constitute 30% to 60% of total education outlays. Always input realistic additional expenses in the calculator for comprehensive planning.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3 class="faq-question">Are the calculator's results guaranteed financial outcomes?</h3>
            <p class="faq-answer">
              No. All projections are educational mathematical models based on the inflation and return assumptions you supply. Actual tuition fees, currency fluctuations, market returns, and institutional policies vary over time and cannot be guaranteed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
